  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense went silent at the worst moments, leaving eight runners on base and taking a 3-0 loss to Tampa Bay.",
    "what_this_means": "Cole was sharp through six innings. The bullpen held clean after him. What didn't happen. What couldn't happen. Was converting a single scoring chance. Two runners in the second. Two more in the seventh. Nothing. Eleven strikeouts silenced the lineup when it mattered most. This isn't the product of bad luck or close calls. This is a team that had the game and didn't finish it, and it cost them a full game in the division race while they continue to slip further from a position they need to own.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tampa Bay did exactly what it needed to do: score early on Cole and then make the bats swing and miss. The Yankees cooperated. They didn't score once, which means six hits and solid pitching weren't enough to matter. That's the difference between holding a team and winning it. Execution in the sixth and seventh inning when runners are standing on base. The window closed because nobody stepped through it.",
